1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:12,633
Poor conditioning forces a trade-off. A step small enough for the stiff direction crawls along the soft one.

2
00:00:12,633 --> 00:00:19,266
A larger step makes the stiff direction overshoot and bounce. Averaging recent gradients could calm it.

3
00:00:19,266 --> 00:00:25,500
Keep a running average: shrink the old average by beta, and add a small dose of the newest gradient.

4
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:32,466
Unrolled, that is a weighted sum of all past gradients, weights falling by a factor beta each step back.

5
00:00:32,466 --> 00:00:42,333
For beta of 0.9, the newest gradient gets weight 0.1, the one before 0.09, and so on. One vector holds it all.

6
00:00:42,333 --> 00:00:50,600
Why not average everything equally? A stale, huge gradient would linger for ages; exponential weights forget.

7
00:00:50,600 --> 00:00:58,100
An electrical low-pass filter, a resistor and a capacitor, stores one state. A sudden step charges it smoothly.

8
00:00:58,100 --> 00:01:05,366
Momentum is the discrete version. If the gradient jumps to one, the average becomes one minus beta to the t.

9
00:01:05,366 --> 00:01:13,366
With beta of 0.9 the memory is about 9 steps: the average needs around ten steps to catch up.

10
00:01:13,366 --> 00:01:20,500
Feed in a gradient with a steady part, one, plus a bounce that flips sign each step, like a stiff direction.

11
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:27,166
The average rises to the steady value and the bounce nearly disappears. That is a low-pass filter at work.

12
00:01:27,166 --> 00:01:34,533
A persistent gradient passes with gain one. An alternating one is cut to one minus beta over one plus beta.

13
00:01:34,533 --> 00:01:43,633
For beta of 0.9, that is five percent. The trial form A times minus one to the t confirms the ratio.

14
00:01:43,633 --> 00:01:50,366
Many libraries omit the one minus beta factor and accumulate an unnormalized velocity instead.

15
00:01:50,366 --> 00:01:58,100
That velocity is bigger by exactly one over one minus beta, so its learning rate must shrink by the same factor.

16
00:01:58,100 --> 00:02:08,366
With beta of 0.9, a rate of 0.1 in one form matches 0.01 in the other. Match before comparing rates.

17
00:02:08,366 --> 00:02:15,833
Here is a ravine: steep across, shallow along. Plain gradient descent with a stable step zigzags across.

18
00:02:15,833 --> 00:02:22,700
With the averaged gradient, the bounces across the ravine cancel while the steady push along it accumulates.

19
00:02:22,700 --> 00:02:32,100
Caution: this shows filtering. A square-root-of-kappa guarantee needs tuning and a smooth, strongly convex loss.

